		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=1620#p1620"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Its best just to stick with a fixed timestep of 60 hertz.<br><br>You can decouple the graphics rendering from the physics timestep, by interpolating (based on velocity), to allow variable framerates.<br><br>It's probably better to optimize the physics so it runs fine at 60 hertz. How many active objects do you have?</div></blockquote>Presumably this advice is talking about some presumed 'normal' range of object sizes and masses.  To take an extreme example, if you were simulating something like two galaxies colliding - then a timestep of a million years might be about right.<br><br>For objects of 'human scale', I think 1/60th is OK - but when you try to work with smaller, lighter objects, you need smaller steps and for larger, heavier objects a larger timestep should suffice.<br><br>...or is there something I misunderstand here?<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=726">SteveBaker</a> — Thu Aug 31, 2006 12:39 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
